E-3 / T2.10 -- window reductions: three interface axes
======================================================

Gates T2.6's interface and T3.6's implementation. T3.6 cannot start until this
closes. Compares binCV against binCV throughout, so ARCHITECTURE 10.3's OpenCV
denominator does not apply to any of the three axes.

DECISION RULES, ALL WRITTEN BEFORE MEASURING (TASKS.md T2.10, recorded in
EXPERIMENTS.md X-11 at commit 4245210, and in benchmark/window_benchmark.cpp):
  AXIS 1  recompute within 15% of incremental at 31x31 -> keep the simpler
          recompute API, close E-3, record that incremental state was rejected on
          data. Incremental wins by > 15% at 31x31 -> extend T2.6 with incremental
          state BEFORE T3.6 is written against the simpler form.
  AXIS 2  a covariance-shaped entry point beats the composition by > 15% at 31x31
          -> add it to T2.6 before T3.6 is written; within 15% -> keep the
          composition and record that the fused form was rejected on data.
  AXIS 3  NO numeric threshold exists. Both memory and speed must be reported,
          and the weighing is against CLAUDE.md's tiebreak -- memory wins when the
          goals conflict and no explicit choice has been made.

PLATFORM: the reference device -- AUTHORITATIVE. Every variant here is
popcount-bound except INC-COL, which issues none, so the X-7 lowering is the
whole ratio and x86 cannot rank these at all.

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0  (every run)
  benchmark commit: 3383996

THREE RUNS. Batch spread within each run was 0.0-2.2% on every row, so the
run-to-run agreement below is the real reproducibility check.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AXIS 1 -- recompute versus two sliding accumulators, uint32_t, 640x480
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
INC-COL is the separable box accumulator: per-column running sums, no popcount at
all, needs a caller-provided scratch array. INC-ROW slides vertically with ONE
scalar accumulator, stays word-parallel, needs no scratch.

Three access patterns, all of them in the MVP:
  SPARSE  200 isolated keypoints          (LK covariance, ARCHITECTURE 7.5)
  SEARCH  200 keypoints x an 8x8 sweep    (heavy local overlap, 12800 windows)
  DENSE   every window position in frame  (corner response, ARCHITECTURE 7.6)

  ns/window, run 3 (runs 1 and 2 agree to <2% on every cell):

  pattern  W    recompute   INC-COL   INC-ROW   INC-COL x   INC-ROW x   scratch
  SPARSE    7        67.9     251.8      69.1       0.27x       0.98x      28 B
  SEARCH    7        47.2      18.1      13.2       2.61x       3.58x      56 B
  DENSE     7        44.9       6.9       8.8       6.48x       5.11x    2560 B
  SPARSE   15       138.0     976.3     118.9       0.14x       1.16x      60 B
  SEARCH   15       110.8      39.7      20.4       2.79x       5.44x      88 B
  DENSE    15       106.7       7.1      10.2      15.06x      10.47x    2560 B
  SPARSE   31       314.1    3879.6     238.1       0.08x       1.32x     124 B
  SEARCH   31       269.8     104.1      36.6       2.59x       7.37x     152 B
  DENSE    31       267.3       7.4      13.2      36.35x      20.27x    2560 B

  the same ratios across runs, at W = 31:
    SPARSE INC-ROW   1.31x  1.32x  1.32x
    SEARCH INC-ROW   7.31x  7.33x  7.37x
    DENSE  INC-COL  36.37x 36.35x 36.35x

READING AXIS 1. At 31x31 an accumulator beats recompute in every pattern, by
1.31x where windows barely overlap and by 7.4x-36x where they do. That is past
the 15% line in all three, so the rule selects its second branch: EXTEND T2.6
WITH INCREMENTAL STATE BEFORE T3.6 IS WRITTEN.

Which accumulator wins depends on the pattern, and the two are not
interchangeable:
  * DENSE favours INC-COL (36x), because it issues no popcount at all and its
    cost per window is nearly constant in W -- 6.9, 7.1 and 7.4 ns at W = 7, 15
    and 31, against recompute's 44.9, 106.7 and 267.3.
  * SPARSE punishes INC-COL brutally (0.08x at W=31): with nothing to slide
    across, it pays W*W bit reads to build state it then discards once.
  * SEARCH and SPARSE favour INC-ROW, which stays word-parallel and keeps no
    array. INC-ROW never loses (0.98x at its worst) and needs no scratch, so it
    is the safer shape for an API that cannot know the caller's access pattern.

*** THE SPARSE ROW IS NOT AN INCREMENTAL WIN, AND THIS IS THE SURPRISE. ***
At SPARSE the sweep is 1x1, so INC-ROW's sliding path never executes: it issues
exactly the same popcounts over exactly the same masked words as the shipped
countNonZero, and it is still 1.31x faster at W=31, 1.16x at 15, 0.98x at 7. The
only difference between the two is where the sum accumulates. impl::countViewRegion
carries ONE size_t total across every row and word of the region, so the whole
traversal is a single serialized dependency chain through the popcount latency;
INC-ROW accumulates per row and adds the rows up, giving the core W independent
chains. The gain grows with W exactly as a dependency-chain explanation predicts.
That is a finding about the shipped reduction's codegen, not about incremental
state, and it is worth more than it looks: it is available to every region
reduction in ops/reduce.hpp without any interface change at all.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AXIS 2 -- the 2x2 covariance composed out of T2.6 versus one fused pass
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ns/keypoint, 200 keypoints, composed / fused:

  word       W    composed   fused    run 1   run 2   run 3
  uint32_t    7      193.7   152.9    1.26x   1.27x   1.27x
  uint32_t   15      432.6   344.0    1.26x   1.25x   1.26x
  uint32_t   31     1011.4   796.3    1.27x   1.27x   1.27x
  uint64_t    7      200.3   144.6    1.38x   1.39x   1.38x
  uint64_t   15      390.7   300.0    1.30x   1.31x   1.30x
  uint64_t   31      822.3   637.0    1.29x   1.29x   1.29x

READING AXIS 2. 1.27x (uint32_t) and 1.29x (uint64_t) at 31x31, past the 15%
line, in all three runs. This reproduces X-8's 1.30x at uint64_t/31x31 in a
separate session and extends it to two word widths and three window sizes, where
it holds from 1.25x to 1.39x. The rule selects: ADD A COVARIANCE-SHAPED ENTRY
POINT TO T2.6 BEFORE T3.6 IS WRITTEN. The composition's three calls visit each
region word three times and load it three times; the popcount count is identical
on both sides, so the delta is redundant traversal.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AXIS 3 -- frame-sized selector plane versus a four-argument countAndSplit
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ns/keypoint, uint32_t, 200 keypoints:

  W     plane (shipped)   four-arg XOR   plane/4arg   run 1   run 2   run 3
   7             109.4          152.1        0.72x    0.74x   0.78x   0.72x
  15             247.4          328.3        0.75x    0.77x   0.80x   0.75x
  31             580.3          731.9        0.79x    0.79x   0.80x   0.79x

  SPEED, per frame at W=31 and 200 keypoints, formation included:
    plane     580.3 ns x 200 = 116.1 us  +  6.5 us to form the plane  = 122.6 us
    four-arg  731.9 ns x 200 = 146.4 us  +  0                         = 146.4 us
    -> the plane is 16% faster per frame per pyramid level, even after paying to
       build it. The four-argument form loads a fourth stream and XORs it inside
       the word loop, and that costs more than one streaming pass over the frame.

  MEMORY:
    selector plane          38400 B per pyramid level (one bit per pixel at 640x480)
    four-argument form          0 B
    for scale: the ternary dx and dy the covariance reads are 4 planes = 153600 B
    per level, so the selector is a fifth plane -- +25% on the derivative working
    set of every level, held for the frame's lifetime.

READING AXIS 3. The two goals disagree, which is exactly why T2.10 set no
threshold here: the plane is 16% faster per frame and costs 38400 B per level;
the four-argument form is 16% slower and costs nothing. CLAUDE.md's tiebreak is
explicit for this situation -- "performance and memory footprint are co-equal
goals; when they conflict and no explicit choice has been made, memory wins" --
so on the project's own stated rule the FOUR-ARGUMENT FORM is the one to build,
buying 38400 B per level (a quarter of the derivative footprint) for 16% of one
operation's time. That weighing is recorded here rather than enacted: adding the
overload is T2.6 API work, and it lands with the axis 1 and axis 2 changes.

X-7 CAVEAT. Every variant above except INC-COL is popcount-bound, and INC-COL is
the one that issues none -- so the axis 1 ratios in particular are a direct
function of what a popcount costs relative to a load on this core. On x86_64,
where __builtin_popcountll is a library call per word, INC-COL's advantage would
be inflated and the ranking would not transfer. No -march flag was added: that is
a dispatch decision (ROADMAP 2.3) no experiment has settled.

